# yaml-language-server: $schema=../schemas/workflow.schema.json

name: Queue Orchestrate
description: >-
  Continuous queue-driven orchestration: pulls tasks from `bd ready`,
  assigns them to worker agents running the grind workflow, hands off
  completed work to a persistent review agent, and manages the full
  lifecycle until the queue is empty.

  Agent roles:
  - Orchestrator (this workflow): queue management, assignment, review
    delegation, merge/close, progress tracking
  - Worker agents (grind.yaml): claim, triage, implement, signal review
  - Review agent (review-implementation.yaml): forensic code review,
    verdict posting

  The orchestrator runs from the main checkout (read-only). Workers and
  reviewers operate in their own worktrees.

  Chat channel: `grind` — all pipeline communication flows here.

  Label conventions:
  - grind-implementing: worker actively implementing
  - grind-review-pending: awaiting review
  - grind-review-approved: review passed, merged
  - grind-review-changes: review found issues, rework needed
  - grind-stale: triaged as stale, skipped

  Worker count: unlimited. The orchestrator assigns one task per worker
  at a time. When a worker finishes (review approved or stale skip),
  it receives the next available task from the queue.

preflight:
  # Step 1: Assess the queue
  - prompt: >-
      Load the work queue and assess scope:

      1. Run `bd ready --json` to get the priority-sorted queue.
      2. Count total ready items.
      3. Group by priority: P0 (critical), P1 (high), P2 (medium), P3 (low).
      4. Check for any items already labelled grind-implementing or
         grind-review-pending from a previous interrupted session.

      Present the queue summary to the user:
        "Queue: N items ready (P0: X, P1: Y, P2: Z, P3: W)"
        "In-progress from previous session: M items" (if any)

      Ask the user how many worker agents they want to start.
      Record: QUEUE_SIZE, WORKER_COUNT, IN_PROGRESS_ITEMS.

      Calculate reviewer count using the auto-scaling formula:
        REVIEWER_COUNT = max(1, ceil(WORKER_COUNT / 2))
      Present to the user:
        "Reviewers: <REVIEWER_COUNT> (auto-scaled: ceil(workers/2), min 1)"
      If QUEUE_SIZE >= 6, note that dynamic scaling may add reviewers later.
      Record: REVIEWER_COUNT.
    category: discovery

  # Step 2: Set up the grind channel
  - prompt: >-
      Initialise the grind coordination channel:

      1. Post the session kickoff message to the grind channel:
         "GRIND SESSION START
         Queue: <QUEUE_SIZE> items
         Workers: <WORKER_COUNT> expected
         Reviewers: <REVIEWER_COUNT> expected (auto-scaled)
         Orchestrator online. Waiting for agents to register."

      2. If there are IN_PROGRESS_ITEMS from a previous session,
         post their status so workers and reviewers can pick them up.

  # Step 3: Generate agent prompts
  - prompt: >-
      Generate onboarding prompts for each agent.

      FOR EACH WORKER AGENT (1 through WORKER_COUNT):

        Each worker should be a **Starbug** agent (select from the agent
        picker in VS Code). Starbug already handles TDD implementation,
        delegation to Haiku/Sonnet subagents, and worktree isolation.

        Generate a bootstrap prompt for each worker that includes:

        a. TELLS THE USER: "Open a new chat window, select the
           **Starbug** agent from the agent picker, and paste
           this prompt."
        b. BOOTSTRAP PROMPT:
           "You are Grind Worker <N> for this session. Execute
           workflows/grind.yaml in a loop. Bootstrap:
             1. Register: session get --name \"Worker-<N>\"
             2. Read channel: chat read --channel grind --last 20
             3. Announce: chat post --channel grind --message \"Worker-<N> online. Standing by.\"
             4. Wait for ASSIGN messages from the orchestrator.
           Follow grind.yaml steps for each assignment. Post status
           updates using the message formats defined in grind.yaml.
           After each task cycle (approved or skipped), wait for next
           ASSIGN. If no ASSIGN arrives within 5 minutes, post:
           \"Worker-<N> idle. Awaiting assignment.\"
           ALL idle time uses: chat wait --channel grind --timeout 0 --interval 10"
        c. MODEL RECOMMENDATION: Starbug runs on Opus 4.6 by default
           and delegates implementation to Sonnet/Haiku subagents when
           cost-efficient — no model override needed.

      FOR EACH REVIEW AGENT (Reviewer-1 through Reviewer-REVIEWER_COUNT):

        The review agent has a dedicated agent definition at
        `.agents/agents/grind-reviewer.agent.md`. Generate a prompt
        for each reviewer that includes:

        a. TELLS THE USER: "Open a new chat window, select the
           **Grind Reviewer** agent from the agent picker, and paste
           this prompt."
        b. BOOTSTRAP PROMPT:
           "You are Reviewer-<N> for this grind session. Execute your
           boot protocol from Section 2 of your agent definition:
           register your session as Reviewer-<N>, read the grind
           channel, and announce yourself. Then wait for
           REVIEW-REQUEST messages directed to you. The orchestrator
           will assign reviews to specific reviewers."
        c. MODEL RECOMMENDATION: Opus-class model recommended for
           deep reasoning during review. The user selects the model
           in the chat window — the agent definition does not lock it.

      Present all prompts to the user clearly labelled. The user will
      copy-paste each into a separate chat window.

      Wait for the user to confirm all agent windows are open.
    category: delivery

  # Step 4: Wait for agent registration
  - prompt: >-
      Wait for all agents to register on the grind channel:

      1. Monitor the channel for "online" messages:
         chat wait --channel grind --timeout 300 --interval 10

      2. Track registrations on a checklist:
         - Worker-1 through Worker-N
         - Reviewer-1 through Reviewer-<REVIEWER_COUNT>

      3. After each wait cycle, read and check:
         chat read --channel grind --last 20

      4. If an agent has not appeared after 5 minutes, ask the user
         to check that agent's chat window.

      5. Once ALL agents are registered, post:
         "ALL AGENTS REGISTERED. Beginning queue processing."
    category: delivery

steps:
  # Step 1: Assignment loop
  - prompt: >-
      Run the main assignment and monitoring loop. This step continues
      until the queue is empty and all in-flight work is complete.

      ASSIGNMENT LOGIC:

      1. Identify idle workers — workers that have:
         a. Just registered and have no assignment, OR
         b. Posted ACK for a completed/skipped task, OR
         c. Posted "idle" message

      2. For each idle worker:
         a. Run `bd ready --json` to get the current queue head.
         b. If the queue is empty and no work is in-flight, go to
            EXIT CONDITION.
         c. If queue is empty but work is in-flight (other workers
            still implementing or reviews pending), tell the idle
            worker: "WAIT Worker-<N>: Queue empty, pending in-flight
            work. Stand by."
         d. If queue has items, pick the highest-priority unassigned item.
            Check it is not already labelled grind-implementing.
         e. DESCRIPTION QUALITY CHECK: Scan the candidate issue's
            description for specific file paths (patterns like *.tsx,
            *.ts, *.py, *.yaml), tool names, or API references. If the
            description contains no file-specific references, append a
            note to the ASSIGN message:
            "NOTE: Description lacks specific file/tool names. Worker
            should update the description during triage."
         f. OVERLAP CHECK (before assigning):
            Before posting the assignment, cross-check the candidate
            issue against all currently in-flight work:

            i.   Get in-flight issues: all items labelled
                 grind-implementing (tracked in your assignment state).
            ii.  For each in-flight issue, extract file/component
                 references from its description (look for patterns
                 like *.tsx, *.ts, *.py, component names, directory
                 paths, and module names).
            iii. Do the same for the candidate issue description.
            iv.  If any extracted paths or component names overlap:
                 - If the overlap is on the SAME file:
                   DEFER the candidate. Skip it for now, pick the
                   next queue item instead. Post:
                   "DEFER <issue-id>: Overlaps with in-flight
                   <other-issue-id> on <file>. Serializing."
                 - If the overlap is on the same DIRECTORY but
                   different files: WARN in the assignment message:
                   "ASSIGN Worker-<N>: <issue-id> -- <title>
                   WARNING: Shares directory with in-flight
                   <other-issue-id>. Coordinate via chat if needed."
            v.   If no overlap, proceed normally.

         g. Post assignment:
            "ASSIGN Worker-<N>: <issue-id> — <title>"
            For complex/architectural tasks, add:
            "NOTE: This task may benefit from Opus-level reasoning."

      MONITORING LOGIC:

      After each assignment round, enter the monitoring loop:

      1. Wait for messages:
         chat wait --channel grind --timeout 120 --interval 10

      2. Read new messages:
         chat read --channel grind

      3. DISPATCH based on message type:

         "TRIAGE-OK <issue-id>":
           - Acknowledge. Worker proceeds with implementation.

         "STALE <issue-id>: <reason>":
           - Verify the staleness assessment. Check bd show for context.
           - If confirmed stale:
             a. Post: "SKIP <issue-id>: Confirmed stale. <reason>"
             b. Close the issue: bd close <issue-id> --reason "Stale: <reason>"
           - If not actually stale:
             a. Post: "OVERRIDE <issue-id>: Not stale. <counter-reason>. Proceed."

         "PLAN <issue-id>: <summary>":
           - Acknowledge. No action needed unless the plan is concerning.

         "START <issue-id>":
           - Track: Worker-N is now implementing <issue-id>.

         "BLOCKER <issue-id>: <details>":
           - Assess the blocker.
           - If resolvable with guidance, post advice.
           - If the worker needs to skip, post: "SKIP <issue-id>: <reason>"
             and file a follow-up issue.
           - If a different worker should take over, reassign.

         "REVIEW-READY <issue-id>: <details>":
           - Delegate to an available reviewer:
             "REVIEW-REQUEST <issue-id>: <branch>
             Worker: <worker-name>
             Summary: <approach-summary>"
           - Track: <issue-id> is now in review.

         "REVIEW-VERDICT <issue-id>: APPROVED":
           NOTE: The reviewer now owns the landing sequence (merge,
           push, close, cleanup). The orchestrator waits for the
           LANDED message confirming completion.

         "LANDED <issue-id>":
           - Acknowledge: the reviewer has merged to main, closed
             the issue, deleted the branch, and cleaned up worktrees.
           - Pull the merged changes locally:
             git pull --rebase (so subsequent merges have the latest main)
           - Notify the worker:
             "REVIEW-APPROVED <issue-id>"
           - The worker will clean up and wait for next assignment.

         "LANDED-BLOCKED <issue-id>: <reason>":
           - The reviewer's landing sequence hit an obstacle (merge
             conflict, push rejection, etc.)
           - Assess the reason and resolve manually if possible.
           - If a merge conflict: resolve locally, push, then close
             the issue and clean up on the reviewer's behalf.
           - Post "REVIEW-APPROVED <issue-id>" once resolved.

         "REVIEW-VERDICT <issue-id>: CHANGES-NEEDED. <findings>":
           - Update labels:
             bd label remove <issue-id> grind-review-pending
             bd label add <issue-id> grind-review-changes
           - Relay feedback to the worker:
             "REVIEW-CHANGES <issue-id>: <findings>"
           - Track: <issue-id> returns to implementation.
           - The worker addresses feedback and re-signals REVIEW-READY.

         "Worker-<N> idle":
           - Trigger a new assignment round for that worker.

      4. PROGRESS TRACKING: After every 3 dispatched events, post a
         progress summary to the grind channel:
         "PROGRESS: <completed>/<total> done. <in-flight> in-flight.
         <queue-remaining> queued. <stale-skipped> skipped."

      5. REVIEWER AUTO-SCALING: After each progress check, evaluate
         whether the reviewer pool needs to scale:
         a. Count current active reviewers (agents that have posted
            to the grind channel and are not stood down).
         b. Count items labelled grind-review-pending (review backlog).
         c. If review backlog >= 2 and active reviewers < ceil(WORKER_COUNT/2),
            or if remaining queue items >= 6 and active reviewers == 1:
            - Post to grind channel:
              "SCALE-UP: Review backlog or queue depth warrants an
              additional reviewer. User: please open a new Grind
              Reviewer window with the Reviewer-<N+1> prompt."
            - Post the reviewer bootstrap prompt (same format as
              preflight Step 3) so the user can copy-paste it.
         d. Do not scale beyond ceil(WORKER_COUNT / 2) reviewers
            unless the review backlog exceeds 3 items.

      6. DEAD WORKER DETECTION: Every 3rd monitoring cycle (roughly
         every 6 minutes), scan for stalled workers:
         a. For each task labelled grind-implementing, check when the
            assigned worker last posted to the grind channel.
         b. If no message from that worker in the last 10 minutes,
            ping them: "HEALTH-CHECK Worker-<N>: Are you still
            working on <issue-id>?"
         c. If no response within 3 minutes of the ping, treat the
            worker as lost:
            - Release the task: bd update <issue-id> --assignee ""
              --status open
            - Remove label: bd label remove <issue-id> grind-implementing
            - Post: "REASSIGN <issue-id>: Worker-<N> unresponsive.
              Task returned to queue."
            - Assign to the next idle worker, or leave in queue.

      7. Repeat the monitoring loop.

      EXIT CONDITION:
      The loop ends when ALL of:
        a. bd ready --json returns zero items
        b. No issues are labelled grind-implementing
        c. No issues are labelled grind-review-pending
        d. All workers have posted ACK for their last assignment

      When exit condition is met, proceed to postflight.
    category: delivery

postflight:
  # Step 1: Sign off workers
  - prompt: >-
      Signal all agents to shut down:

      1. Post to grind channel:
         "GRIND SESSION COMPLETE.
         Total processed: <count>
         Approved and merged: <count>
         Skipped (stale): <count>
         All workers: stand down. Push any remaining commits and
         clean up worktrees."

      2. Wait for each worker to acknowledge:
         chat wait --channel grind --timeout 120 --interval 10

      3. Instruct all reviewers:
         "All reviewers: session complete. No more reviews incoming. Stand down."

  # Step 2: Final sync
  - prompt: >-
      Sync all state:

      1. Run `bd dolt pull && bd dolt push`
      2. Verify git status: all branches merged, main is clean.
      3. Post final summary to all-agents channel:
         "GRIND SESSION ENDED.
         Processed: <total>
         Merged: <merged>
         Skipped: <skipped>
         Duration: <start-to-end>"

  # Step 3: File follow-up work
  - prompt: >-
      Review whether any follow-up issues need to be filed:

      1. Check for WARNING findings from reviews that were not
         filed as issues during the session.
      2. Check for any tasks that were skipped due to blockers
         that need re-queuing.
      3. File any necessary follow-up issues with bd create.
    condition: There are untracked follow-up items from the session.

hooks:
  beforeEach:
    prompt: >-
      Before each main step:
      1. Read the grind channel for any new messages:
         chat read --channel grind
      2. Check for urgent messages (BLOCKER, agent failures).
      3. If urgent messages exist, handle them before continuing
         the current step.
